=== GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sh4_pcic_probe.py ===
1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:"""#443 DETECTOR: an unsupported SH-4 PCIC transaction is DIAGNOSED AND SURVIVED
3:instead of calling exit(1) -- and, just as importantly, is not silently absorbed.
4:
5:Rung 3.  Real SH-4 guest instructions through real address decode, real
6:`memory_rw` and real device dispatch, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree `-E landisk`.
7:No source is edited, no `device_add` of our own, no boot.
8:
9:WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  The pre-fix WITNESS (`_scratchpad/sh4pcic_witness.py`)
10:asserted the SYMPTOM -- a MEASURED census of all 137 word offsets in both
11:directions found 116 killing the host on a plain 32-bit READ and 125 on a write
12:-- so it goes red once the defect is gone, which is what a witness should do.
13:This file is the DETECTOR: it asserts the FIXED behaviour, fails on the pre-fix
14:build, and is built to fail on the mutants listed at the foot of this docstring.
15:Grading one by the other's clauses is a category error in either direction.
16:
17:*** THE ROW THAT MATTERS MOST IS P3, NOT P1. ***  Deleting `exit(1)` and nothing
18:else does not fix this defect -- it makes it worse.  The store into `pcic_reg[]`
19:at dev_sh4.c:875-879 is UPSTREAM of every guard, so a rejected value has ALREADY
20:landed by the time the guard runs, and `exit(1)` was the only thing that stopped
21:a guest reading it back.  P3 writes an accepted value, then a rejected one, then
22:READS THE REGISTER BACK through the guest; the naive fix turns a host kill into
23:silent state corruption, and P3 is the only row in this file that sees it.
24:[#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] silent state corruption, seen
25:by P3, P16, and P17.  (hsh4pcicprobe:F6: regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
26:
27:P3b is its sibling and covers the `default:` arm, whose restore is otherwise
28:UNOBSERVABLE: nothing else in the tree reads `pcic_reg[]`, and a `default:` read
29:answers 0 regardless.  It becomes observable through ADDRESS ALIASING -- the
30:switch dispatches on the FULL address, but PCIC_REG() indexes on the WORD, so
31:0xfe200015 (a byte inside PCICONF5) reaches `default:` while sharing PCICONF5's
32:array slot.  A byte store there clobbers the whole word on the way in; only the
33:restore puts it back.
34:
35:P2b uses the same aliasing in the other direction, and it is what gives "reads
36:answer 0" any teeth at all: on a fresh device every unimplemented slot already
37:holds 0, and writes to unimplemented offsets are dropped, so `odata = 0` and
38:`odata = pcic_reg[i]` agree EVERYWHERE until a labelled register has put a
39:non-zero word into a slot that `default:` can reach.
40:
41:*** SEVEN MUTANTS PASSED THE FIRST 29 ROWS.  P14-P20 ARE WHAT CLOSES THEM. ***
42:The 29-row file measured ONE access width, observed TWO of the ten `pcic_old`
43:restores, never offered any arm a SECOND rejected write, and never wrote
44:PCICONF0 before reading it.  Each of those blanks was a family of survivors,
45:not a single mutant:
46:
47:  P14/P15  the WIDTH AXIS.  `if (len == 2) exit(1);` -- the original host kill
48:           narrowed to one width -- passed 29/29, because len=2 was issued
49:           NOWHERE in the file and len=1 at only two fixed addresses.
50:  P16      one read-back per RESTORE.  Deleting a single restore (PCIMBR's,
51:           say) passed 29/29 and let a guest read its own rejected word back.
52:  P17/P18  the SECOND OFFENCE.  Bracing the restore INSIDE the latch -- a
53:           brace slip, the shape a tidy-up produces -- restores the first
54:           offence and silently corrupts every one after it.  29/29, ten arms.
55:  P19      the MUTUALLY-MASKING PAIR in the PCICONF0 arm.  Each of the two
56:           statements is void alone, so single-line mutation is blind to both;
57:           delete both and the guest gets its own word back.
58:  P20      the latch keyed on (offset, FAULT CLASS).  A per-offset latch merges
59:           two different faults that share one array slot and drops the second
60:           diagnostic entirely.
61:
62:P4 counts the LATCHED SUFFIX, never a register name.  Under a cold debugger
63:`single_step` is true, so `debug()`'s quiet_mode early-out never fires and a
64:probe run this way CANNOT distinguish `fatal()` from `debug()` by presence -- a
65:#441 detector row was passed by a two-character mutant for exactly that reason.
66:P5 is P4's other half: a PER-DEVICE latch passes P4 and fails P5.
67:
68:NO SH-4 MANUAL IS CITED ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, and every constant below is read
69:out of a header in this repository -- with the file and line named -- or measured.
70:
71:*** THE REASON GIVEN FOR THAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-22, AND THE PRACTICE DID NOT. ***
72:This paragraph used to say there was no SH-4 manual in the tree, which was true
73:when it was written (`_scratchpad/sh4_manual.txt` is one byte) and is FALSE now:
74:a real SH7750 Hardware Manual (Rev.7.00, REJ09B0366-0700) and SH-4 Software
75:Manual were obtained by owner directive and extracted to
76:`_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` and `_scratchpad/sh4_programming.txt`.
77:
78:The file still cites none, deliberately.  A probe's constants should come from
79:the headers the CODE actually compiles against, because a probe that agrees with
80:the manual while the code disagrees with both would be green and useless.  The
81:manual is now available for deciding what the code OUGHT to do; the headers
82:remain the authority on what it DOES.
83:
84:usage:  sh4_pcic_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <landisk-kernel> [--jobs N] [--fast]
85:
86:    *** KEEP THE "./" ON THE BINARY. ***  `os.execvp` on a bare name searches
87:    PATH; when it misses, EVERY arm silently reports alive=False and a probe
88:    written the naive way "passes" having measured nothing.  P0 catches that,
89:    and the existence check in main() catches it sooner.
90:"""
91:import argparse
92:import os
93:import pty
94:import re
95:import select
96:import sys
97:import time
98:from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
99:
100:# ----------------------------------------------------------------- constants
101:#  All READ from this tree.  Every one was opened.
102:SH4_PCIC = 0xfe200000               # src/include/thirdparty/sh4_pcicreg.h:39
103:N_PCIC_REGS = 0x224 // 4            # src/devices/dev_sh4.c:72        -> 137
104:
105:#  The 21 offsets carrying a `case` label in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_pcic),
106:#  dev_sh4.c:885..1036 (`default:` at :1041).  Names from sh4_pcicreg.h:48..110.
107:LABELLED = {
108:    0x000: "PCICONF0", 0x004: "PCICONF1", 0x008: "PCICONF2",
109:    0x014: "PCICONF5", 0x018: "PCICONF6",
110:    0x100: "PCICR",    0x104: "PCILSR0",  0x108: "PCILSR1",
111:    0x10c: "PCILAR0",  0x110: "PCILAR1",
112:    0x1c0: "PCIPAR",   0x1c4: "PCIMBR",   0x1c8: "PCIIOBR",
113:    0x1e0: "PCIBCR1",  0x1e4: "PCIBCR2",  0x1e8: "PCIWCR1", 0x1ec: "PCIWCR2",
114:    0x1f0: "PCIWCR3",  0x1f4: "PCIMCR",   0x1f8: "PCIBCR3",
115:    0x220: "PCIPDR",
116:}
117:ALL_OFFS = list(range(0, N_PCIC_REGS * 4, 4))
118:UNLABELLED = sorted(o for o in ALL_OFFS if o not in LABELLED)
119:
120:#  The nine labelled offsets whose WRITE arm carries a value guard, each with
121:#  the substring its diagnostic prints.  dev_sh4.c:885, 929, 941, 953, 965,
122:#  977, 989, 1001, 1013.
123:GUARD_MSG = {
124:    0x000: "write to SH4_PCICONF0",
125:    0x014: "SH4_PCICONF5 unknown value",
126:    0x018: "SH4_PCICONF6 unknown value",
127:    0x104: "SH4_PCILSR0 unknown value",
128:    0x108: "SH4_PCILSR1 unknown value",
129:    0x10c: "SH4_PCILAR0 unknown value",
130:    0x110: "SH4_PCILAR1 unknown value",
131:    0x1c4: "PCIMBR set to",
132:    0x1c8: "PCIIOBR set to",
133:}
134:
135:#  The EIGHT labelled arms that carry BOTH a value guard and a restore of the
136:#  previous word, with the value each one accepts.  Read out of the guard
137:#  expressions in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4_pcic); the two symbolic ones are
138:#  SH4_PCIC_MEM (sh4_pcicreg.h:44) and SH4_PCIC_IO (sh4_pcicreg.h:41).
139:#
140:#  There are TEN restores in that function -- these eight, plus PCICONF0's
141:#  (unobservable by design; see P19) and `default:`'s (P3b, P18).  Before P16
142:#  only TWO of the ten were observed by this file, and deleting any of the
143:#  other eight was MEASURED to pass all 29 of its rows.
144:RESTORE_ARMS = {
145:    0x014: ("PCICONF5", 0xac000000),
146:    0x018: ("PCICONF6", 0x8c000000),
147:    0x104: ("PCILSR0", (64 - 1) << 20),
148:    0x108: ("PCILSR1", (64 - 1) << 20),
149:    0x10c: ("PCILAR0", 0xac000000),
150:    0x110: ("PCILAR1", 0xac000000),
151:    0x1c4: ("PCIMBR", 0xfd000000),
152:    0x1c8: ("PCIIOBR", 0xfe240000),
153:}
154:
155:#  One word every one of those eight arms rejects, and which no arm accepts.
156:REJECT = 0x12345678
157:
158:#  The load/store pair for each ACCESS WIDTH, keyed by `len` as the device sees
159:#  it.  Without this the census was one-dimensional -- see P14/P15.
160:LDST = {
161:    1: (0x6210, 0x2100),        # mov.b @r1,r2  /  mov.b r0,@r1
162:    2: (0x6211, 0x2101),        # mov.w @r1,r2  /  mov.w r0,@r1
163:    4: (0x6212, 0x2102),        # mov.l @r1,r2  /  mov.l r0,@r1
164:}
165:
166:#  Guest scratch.  landisk RAM is 64 MB at 0x0c000000 (machine_landisk.c:84);
167:#  0x8c010000 is its P1 (cached, unmapped) alias, past the 0x8c002000 entry
168:#  point and inside the loaded image -- the same scratch #441's probe uses.
169:CODE = 0x8c010000
170:DEST = 0x8c010100
171:RAMSRC = 0x8c010200
172:
173:#  Instruction halfwords.  EVERY ONE IS CHECKED against the emulator's own
174:#  disassembler in a session that executed it -- row P11.  A wrong register
175:#  field is silent: it yields 0, which every zero-valued oracle accepts by
176:#  accident, which is why P7c also insists on a small NON-ZERO value.
177:#  [#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] Planted halfwords checked
178:#  against the emulator's own disassembler (all executed except 0x0009 nop).  (hsh4pcicprobe:F7:
179:#  regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
180:OPS = {
=== GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sh4_val_probe.py ===
1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:"""#447 DETECTOR: the four value guards in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4) DIAGNOSE AND SURVIVE
3:instead of calling exit(1) -- and do not silently absorb the rejected value.
4:
5:Rung 3.  Real SH-4 guest instructions through real address decode, real
6:`memory_rw` and real device dispatch, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree `-E landisk`.
7:No source is edited, no `device_add` of our own, no boot.
8:
9:WHY THIS IS A SIBLING OF sh4_pcic_probe.py AND NOT MORE ROWS INSIDE IT.  That
10:file is #443's detector for `dev_sh4_pcic_access()`; this one is #447's for
11:`DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4)`.  Different function, different registers, different
12:address space, different latch array, different diagnostics.  gate_sh_rounding
13:grades sh4_pcic_probe's verdict under the name "sh4_pcic: no offset kills the
14:host", and folding #447 rows under that token would make the gate's own check
15:name untrue -- the wrong-record class this project treats as a defect.
16:
17:IT IMPORTS ITS SESSION MACHINERY FROM sh4_pcic_probe RATHER THAN COPYING IT,
18:and that is deliberate in two directions.  It is the first probe in this tree to
19:import another, so it is worth stating why: gate_hygiene.sh counts the three
20:#392 readiness idioms across every regress/*.py and pins the total
21:(EXPECT_CONVERTED), so a COPY would silently need that pin bumped by three, and
22:a copy is also a second place for the predicate to rot.  Importing keeps all
23:three counts unchanged and keeps one correct predicate in the tree.  MEASURED:
24:with this file present the three counts are still 23/23/23 and bare/unknown/
25:whole_full are still 0/0/2.
26:
27:WHAT THE FOUR SITES ARE, and they are NOT interchangeable:
28:
29:  TCR0/1/2   0xffd80010/1c/28   idata & TCR_UNIMPLEMENTED (six bits)
30:  DMATCR0..7 0xffa00008 + ...   idata & ~0x00ffffff
31:  ICR        0xffd00000         idata & 0x80        (IRLM)
32:  RCR1       0xffc80038         idata & 0x18        (CIE|AIE -- TWO features)
33:
34:*** THE ROW THAT MATTERS MOST IS V17, NOT THE SURVIVAL ROWS. ***  Deleting `exit(1)` and
35:nothing else does not fix RCR1 -- it makes it worse.  `d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;` ran
36:BEFORE the guard, so the rejected value had already landed and `exit(1)` was the
37:only thing stopping the guest reading it back.  V17 writes an accepted value,
38:then a rejected one, then READS THE REGISTER BACK through the guest.  The naive
39:fix turns a host kill into silent state corruption and V17 is the row that sees
40:it.  The other three sites do NOT have that shape -- checked per site, not
41:assumed from #443 -- and saying otherwise in a comment would have been wrong
42:three times out of four.
43:
44:*** V19 IS THE VACUITY TRAP THIS FILE WAS BUILT AROUND. ***  A row that reads
45:state back through the same path that wrote it proves nothing if the read path
46:launders the value.  DMATCR's read arm masks with 0x00ffffff, so the rejected
47:top bits are invisible whether they were stored or not; the consumer that would
48:see them, sh4_dmac_transfer(), masks with 0x1fffffff -- FIVE BITS WIDER.  So V19
49:does not look for the top bits at all.  It writes an accepted 0x00000010, then a
50:rejected 0x01abcdef, and demands the LOW 24 bits still read 0x000010.
51:
52:*** THE WIDTH AXIS IS PINNED, BECAUSE #443's CENSUS WAS ONE-DIMENSIONAL. ***
53:That file issued only len=4 and a mutant reading `if (len == 2) exit(1);` passed
54:29 of its 29 rows.  Three of this cluster's four registers are NOT 32-bit --
55:ICR and TCR are 16-bit and RCR1 is 8-bit -- so len=4 is the UNNATURAL width at
56:three of the four sites.  V5-V8 issue every reachable width at every site, in
57:both the tripping and the non-tripping direction, and V9's census pin fails if
58:the table ever shrinks.  len=8 is not covered because it is not reachable: every
59:memory_rw() call in cpu_sh_instr.c passes sizeof(data) with `data` declared
60:uint8_t, uint16_t or uint32_t -- there are no uint64_t declarations in that file
61:-- and SZ=1 register-pair fmov is implemented as two 32-bit accesses at addr and
62:addr+4 (cpu_sh_instr.c:797-798, :1459-1470).
63:[#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] uint64_t locals do exist in
64:cpu_sh_instr.c (line 313); the claim that the file has none is false. Instruction memory_rw() calls still transfer
65:1, 2, or 4 bytes, and SZ=1 register-pair fmov uses two 4-byte accesses at addr and addr+4, so len=8 is not reached.
66:(hsh4valprobe:F3: regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
67:
68:V24-V28 pin the WHOLE diagnostic line, not one token.  #444 pinned a single
69:ARGUMENT and left the format free, and four seats independently found mutants in
70:other fields of the same string.  V28 additionally pins the TCR mask itself by
71:writing all six bits at once: drop any one of them from TCR_UNIMPLEMENTED and
72:the printed value changes.
73:[#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] V24-V28 pin the diagnostic from
74:'[ sh4:' through '-- write ignored.  (once per bit) ]', without anchoring the trailing line end.  (hsh4valprobe:F4:
75:regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
76:
77:V13-V15 are about the LATCH KEY, which is (class, instance, offending bit):
78:  V11/V12  a key WITHOUT the instance reports timer 0 and silences timers 1
79:           and 2, and reports DMA channel 0 and silences the other seven --
80:           #443's measured half-fix, one level down.
81:  V13/V14  a key without the BIT reports the first feature a guest asks for and
82:           swallows every other one AT THE SAME REGISTER.  RCR1's one guard
83:           covers two independent features and TCR's covers six.
84:  V15      the key is the MASKED bits, not the written value.  Keying on the
85:           value would make 0x0004 and 0x0104 two complaints when they are one,
86:           and hand a looping guest an unbounded host-CPU burn -- the exact
87:           thing the latch exists to prevent.  fatal() has no quiet_mode
88:           early-out, so -q could not silence it.
89:
90:V10 is the direction axis: all four guards sit on the WRITE path, so a read at
91:any of the four addresses must survive AND diagnose nothing.
92:
93:Under a cold debugger `single_step` is true, so `debug()`'s quiet_mode early-out
94:never fires and a STEPPED row cannot distinguish `fatal()` from `debug()` by
95:presence.  Every latch row therefore counts the LATCHED SUFFIX, never a register
96:name.
97:
98:*** BUT THAT LIMIT IS ESCAPABLE, AND V32 ESCAPES IT. ***  The standing rule in
99:this project is stated unconditionally -- "a probe cannot tell fatal() from
100:debug()" -- and it is only true WHILE SINGLE-STEPPING.  debugmsg.c:367-373
101:computes `ss = single_step || about_to_enter_single_step` and then
102:`if (emul_executing) v--; if (ss) v++;`, so stepping pushes debug() ABOVE the
103:threshold and free-running pushes it below.  Run the same store under
104:`continue` with a breakpoint after it and debug() goes silent while fatal()
105:does not -- with no -q needed.  MEASURED both ways on two builds; a mutant with
106:all four fatal() calls swapped for debug() scored 32/32 against this file
107:before V32 existed, and V32 is the only row that fails it.
108:
109:NO SH-4 MANUAL IS CITED ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, and every constant below is read
110:out of a header in this repository -- with the file and line named -- or measured.
111:
112:*** THE REASON GIVEN FOR THAT CHANGED ON 2026-08-22, AND THE PRACTICE DID NOT. ***
113:This paragraph used to say there was no SH-4 manual in the tree, which was true
114:when it was written (`_scratchpad/sh4_manual.txt` is one byte) and is FALSE now:
115:a real SH7750 Hardware Manual (Rev.7.00, REJ09B0366-0700) and SH-4 Software
116:Manual were obtained by owner directive and extracted to
117:`_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt` and `_scratchpad/sh4_programming.txt`.
118:
119:The file still cites none, deliberately.  A probe's constants should come from
120:the headers the CODE actually compiles against, because a probe that agrees with
121:the manual while the code disagrees with both would be green and useless.  The
122:manual is now available for deciding what the code OUGHT to do; the headers
123:remain the authority on what it DOES.
124:
125:usage:  sh4_val_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <landisk-kernel>
126:
127:    *** KEEP THE "./" ON THE BINARY. ***  `os.execvp` on a bare name searches
128:    PATH; when it misses, EVERY arm silently reports alive=False and a probe
129:    written the naive way "passes" having measured nothing.  V0 catches that,
130:    and the existence check in main() catches it sooner.
131:"""
132:import argparse
133:import os
134:import re
135:import sys
136:import time
137:
138:#  Python puts this script's own directory on sys.path[0], so the import below
139:#  finds the sibling however the gate spells its cwd.
140:import sh4_pcic_probe as P
141:
142:# ----------------------------------------------------------------- constants
143:#  All READ from this tree.  Every one was opened.
144:SH4_TCR0 = 0xffd80010           # thirdparty/sh4_tmureg.h:54
145:SH4_TCR1 = 0xffd8001c           # :57
146:SH4_TCR2 = 0xffd80028           # :60
147:SH4_DMATCR0 = 0xffa00008        # sh4_dmacreg.h:39
148:SH4_DMATCR1 = 0xffa00018        # :44   -- the stride is 0x10 up to channel 3
149:SH4_DMATCR7 = 0xffa00088        # :84   -- the far end of the fall-through chain
150:SH4_ICR = 0xffd00000            # thirdparty/sh4_intcreg.h:79   ("16bit")
151:SH4_RCR1 = 0xffc80038           # thirdparty/sh4_rtcreg.h:68
152:SH4_PVR_ADDR = 0xff000030       # thirdparty/sh4_cpu.h:187
153:SH4_PRR_ADDR = 0xff000044       # :189
154:SH4_PVR_SH7751 = 0x04110000     # :196   -- what SH7751R's pvr field holds
155:SH4_PRR_7751R = 0x00000110      # :200
156:UNCLAIMED = 0xff00fffc          # no `case` in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4); reaches `default:`
157:
158:#  The six TCR bits DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4) does not model.  thirdparty/sh4_tmureg.h
159:#  :75-82.  TCR_UNF (0x0100) is deliberately NOT here -- it IS modelled, and V15
160:#  uses that fact.
161:TCR_ICPF, TCR_ICPE1, TCR_ICPE0 = 0x0200, 0x0080, 0x0040
162:TCR_CKEG1, TCR_CKEG0, TCR_TPSC2 = 0x0010, 0x0008, 0x0004
163:TCR_UNF = 0x0100
164:TCR_UNIMPLEMENTED = (TCR_ICPF | TCR_ICPE1 | TCR_ICPE0 |
165:                     TCR_CKEG1 | TCR_CKEG0 | TCR_TPSC2)      # 0x02dc
166:TCR_REJECT = TCR_ICPF | TCR_TPSC2                            # 0x0204, V21's
167:
168:#  *** THE BITS AT THE SAME REGISTER THAT ARE MODELLED. ***  A mask pinned only
169:#  from BELOW -- every bit in it is rejected -- cannot see a guard that GREW, and
170:#  a guard that grew takes a working feature away from the guest for good.  These
171:#  three are the ones widening TCR_UNIMPLEMENTED would swallow, and all three are
172:#  live: TCR_UNIE is consumed at dev_sh4.c:313 (`if (d->tcr[i] & TCR_UNIE)`), so
173:#  adding it to the mask means the guest can never enable the timer interrupt;
174:#  TCR_TPSC0 and TCR_TPSC1 are the prescaler switch's own two bits, so adding
175:#  either makes two of the four TPSC codes unusable.  thirdparty/sh4_tmureg.h:79,
176:#  :83-84.
177:TCR_UNIE = 0x0020
178:TCR_TPSC1, TCR_TPSC0 = 0x0002, 0x0001
179:
180:#  The eight-bit window the TCR singleton sweep walks, low bit first -- one
=== GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sh4_rtcsr_witness.py ===
1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:"""`sh4rtcsr` WITNESS (rung 3): a guest RTCSR write ends the host process MILLISECONDS
3:LATER, from a 110 Hz host timer callback -- on an UNMODIFIED in-tree `-E landisk`.
4:
5:*** THIS IS A SHAPE NO OTHER PROBE IN THIS TREE CAN SEE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL. ***
6:
7:Every SH-4 probe here observes the STORE SITE: issue one guest store, then ask whether the
8:host survived that instruction.  #443, #447 and #448 are all built that way, and it is the
9:right shape for them -- their defects die inside `DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4)`, on the store.
10:
11:This one does not.  The write lands, the handler returns, the debugger prompt comes back,
12:and the process is killed afterwards by a callback:
13:
14:    dev_sh4.c   case SH4_RTCSR:  d->bsc_rtcsr = idata & 0x00ff;   <- returns normally
15:    dev_sh4.c   sh4_timer_tick():
16:                    if (d->bsc_rtcsr & (RTCSR_CMIE | RTCSR_OVIE)) {
17:                            fatal("sh4: RTCSR_CMIE | RTCSR_OVIE: TODO");
18:                            exit(1);
19:                    }
20:    dev_sh4.c   d->sh4_timer = timer_add(SH4_PSEUDO_TIMER_HZ, sh4_timer_tick, d);
21:
22:SH4_PSEUDO_TIMER_HZ is 110.0, so the callback fires about every nine milliseconds of free
23:running.  A store-site probe asks its question and gets "alive" -- correctly, and
24:uselessly.  Row S0 below is that observation, kept as a ROW rather than a footnote,
25:because the distance between S0 and S1 IS the finding.
26:
27:HOW THE GUEST IS MADE TO RUN FREE, and why the spin is BOUNDED.  The session helper
28:decides liveness by sending `dump` after the run: an UNBOUNDED spin never returns to the
29:prompt, so `dump` never answers and the row would read `alive=False` on a perfectly
30:healthy host -- a false positive indistinguishable from the defect.  So the sequence is
31:`dt`/`bf` for a fixed count, then a sentinel store, then a breakpoint that returns control.
32:
33:    mov.w r0,@r1   store the RTCSR value
34:    dt    r5       decrement and test
35:    bf    -3       loop while non-zero
36:    mov.l r2,@r3   sentinel: proves the loop RAN TO COMPLETION
37:    nop            breakpoint sits here
38:
39:*** THE SPIN COUNT WAS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN, AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS WITNESS FOUND
40:NOTHING. ***  At 0x60000 and 0x200000 iterations every arm survived -- the guest simply did
41:not run long enough in HOST time for one callback.  0x2000000 kills in about 0.2 s.  A
42:reproduction that finds nothing because it did not wait is not evidence of absence, and
43:the earlier numbers are recorded here so nobody re-derives them.
44:
45:THE CONTROLS ARE WHAT MAKE THIS A MEASUREMENT.  C1 and C2 write the SAME REGISTER at the
46:SAME WIDTH with the SAME spin and the host LIVES, so the result reads "these BITS end the
47:host" rather than "this register does" or "a long free run does".  C2 is the sharper of
48:the two: RTCSR_CMF is a different bit of the same byte.  D1 proves the store reached the
49:DEVICE at all by reading the value back through the same decode.
50:
51:*** A NOTE ON THE READ-BACK ROW, because getting it wrong cost a measurement here. ***
52:The first draft used opcode 0x6121 for `mov.w @r1,r2`.  That encoding is `mov.w @r2,r1` --
53:the operands reversed -- so it read an unrelated register and returned ZERO, which looks
54:exactly like "the store never landed".  The correct encoding is 0x6211.  This is the
55:hand-assembled-encoding trap this project has recorded four times: A WRONG REGISTER FIELD
56:YIELDS ZERO, AND ZERO IS THE VALUE A CARELESS ROW ACCEPTS AS MEANINGFUL.  D1 expects a
57:SMALL NON-ZERO value for exactly that reason.
58:
59:THIS ASSERTS THE PRE-FIX SYMPTOM, so it is RED once the defect is gone, and it must NEVER
60:be wired into a gate -- `check_probe_wiring.py` treats a gated witness as a HARD failure.
61:
62:usage:  sh4_rtcsr_witness.py <gxemul-binary> <landisk-kernel>
63:"""
64:import os
65:import sys
66:
67:import sh4_pcic_probe as P
68:
69:SH4_RTCSR = 0xff80001c          # thirdparty/sh4_bscreg.h:61   (16 bit)
70:RTCSR_CMF = 1 << 7              # :79
71:RTCSR_CMIE = 1 << 6             # :80
72:RTCSR_OVIE = 1 << 1             # :83
73:
74:#  MEASURED: 0x60000 and 0x200000 are both too short -- every arm survives.  0x2000000
75:#  kills in ~0.2 s wall clock.  Left generous rather than trimmed to the edge, because a
76:#  spin tuned to the threshold turns a loaded host into a false green.
77:SPIN = 0x2000000
78:
79:#  mov.w r0,@r1 ; dt r5 ; bf -3 ; mov.l r2,@r3 ; nop
80:OPS = [0x2101, 0x4510, 0x8BFD, 0x2322, 0x0009]
81:SENTINEL = 0x5a5a1234
82:
83:EXPECT_ROWS = 8
84:
85:rows = []
86:
87:
88:def row(name, ok, got, want):
89:    rows.append((name, bool(ok), got, want))
90:    print("  %-4s %s" % ("ok" if ok else "FAIL", name))
91:    if not ok:
92:        print("       got  %s\n       want %s" % (got, want))
93:
94:
95:def freerun(val, label, kw):
96:    """Store `val` to RTCSR, then run free for SPIN iterations."""
97:    return P.session(
98:        ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (P.DEST, P.POISON),
99:         "r0=0x%x" % val, "r1=0x%x" % SH4_RTCSR,
100:         "r5=0x%x" % SPIN, "r2=0x%x" % SENTINEL, "r3=0x%x" % P.DEST]
101:        + P.poke(OPS)
102:        + ["breakpoint add 0x%x" % (P.CODE + 8), "pc=0x%x" % P.CODE, "continue"],
103:        0, label, disasm_upto=0, **dict(kw, timeout=90))
104:
105:
106:def main():
107:    if len(sys.argv) != 3:
108:        print("usage:  sh4_rtcsr_witness.py <gxemul-binary> <landisk-kernel>")
109:        return 2
110:    b, k = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
111:    kw = dict(binary=b, kernel=k)
112:    for p in (b, k):
113:        if not os.path.exists(p):
114:            print("OPERATIONAL FAILURE: %s does not exist (cwd=%s)" % (p, os.getcwd()))
115:            print("SH4RTCSR_WITNESS_FAIL")
116:            return 2
117:
118:    #  ---- D1: the store reaches the DEVICE ------------------------------------------
119:    #  mov.w r0,@r1 ; mov.w @r1,r2 ; mov.l r2,@r3 ; nop   -- note 0x6211, see the header.
120:    buf, alive, st = P.session(
121:        ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (P.DEST, P.POISON),
122:         "r0=0x%x" % RTCSR_CMIE, "r1=0x%x" % SH4_RTCSR, "r3=0x%x" % P.DEST]
123:        + P.poke([0x2101, 0x6211, 0x2322, 0x0009]) + ["pc=0x%x" % P.CODE],
124:        3, "D1", disasm_upto=8, **kw)
125:    v = P.dumped(buf)
126:    row("D1 DEVICE: RTCSR reads back the CMIE bit just written (small NON-ZERO)",
127:        st and alive and v == P.le(RTCSR_CMIE),
128:        "started=%s alive=%s val=%s" % (st, alive, v),
129:        "val=%s -- a wrong register field would answer %s, which is why this row wants "
130:        "a non-zero" % (P.le(RTCSR_CMIE), P.le(0)))
131:
132:    #  ---- S0: THE ROW THAT NAMES THE SHAPE ------------------------------------------
133:    #  *** THE STORE ITSELF IS HARMLESS, AND THAT IS THE POINT. ***  Every other SH-4
134:    #  probe in this tree would stop here and report the host healthy.
135:    buf, alive, st = P.write_arm(SH4_RTCSR, RTCSR_CMIE, "S0", kw, op=0x2101)
136:    row("S0 the STORE returns normally -- a store-site probe sees NOTHING wrong",
137:        st and alive,
138:        "started=%s alive=%s" % (st, alive),
139:        "alive: the handler stores and returns, so the defect is invisible to every "
140:        "probe shape this project has used on this device")
141:
142:    #  ---- S1/S2: the DELAYED kills ---------------------------------------------------
143:    for name, val, bit in [("S1 CMIE 0x40", RTCSR_CMIE, "CMIE"),
144:                           ("S2 OVIE 0x02", RTCSR_OVIE, "OVIE")]:
145:        buf, alive, st = freerun(val, name.split()[0], kw)
146:        seen = "RTCSR_CMIE | RTCSR_OVIE" in (buf or "")
147:        row("%s KILLS the host from the 110 Hz callback, AFTER the store returned"
148:            % name,
149:            st and not alive and seen,
150:            "started=%s alive=%s diagnostic=%s" % (st, alive, seen),
151:            "started, NOT alive, and the timer's own diagnostic present (%s)" % bit)
152:
153:    #  ---- C1/C2: same register, same width, same spin, and the host LIVES ------------
154:    for name, val in [("C1 CONTROL 0x00 -- no interrupt-enable bit", 0x00),
155:                      ("C2 CONTROL 0x80 -- RTCSR_CMF, a DIFFERENT bit of the same byte",
156:                       RTCSR_CMF)]:
157:        buf, alive, st = freerun(val, name.split()[0], kw)
158:        sent = P.dumped(buf)
159:        row("%s -- host SURVIVES the same free run" % name,
160:            st and alive and sent == P.le(SENTINEL),
161:            "started=%s alive=%s sentinel=%s" % (st, alive, sent),
162:            "alive AND sentinel=%s -- the sentinel proves the spin RAN TO COMPLETION, so "
163:            "'survived' is not 'never ran'" % P.le(SENTINEL))
164:
165:    #  ---- W0 / identity ---------------------------------------------------------------
166:    n_ses = len(P.STARTS)
167:    n_ok = sum(1 for _, s in P.STARTS if s)
168:    row("W0 EVERY session reached the debugger prompt (absent data must FAIL)",
169:        n_ses > 0 and n_ok == n_ses,
170:        "sessions=%d started=%d" % (n_ses, n_ok), "all %d started" % n_ses)
171:
172:    row("W-id IDENTITY row count -- guards against a stale copy",
173:        len(rows) + 1 == EXPECT_ROWS, len(rows) + 1, EXPECT_ROWS)
174:
175:    bad = sum(1 for _, ok, _, _ in rows if not ok)
176:    print()
177:    print("SH4RTCSR_WITNESS_RESULT=%d/%d" % (len(rows) - bad, len(rows)))
178:    print("SH4RTCSR_WITNESS_PASS" if bad == 0 else "SH4RTCSR_WITNESS_FAIL")
179:    return 1 if bad else 0
